      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Deerwester\n- 1 -\n1. Introduction\nWe describe here a new approach to automatic indexing and retrieval. It is designed to\novercome a fundamental problem that plagues existing retrieval techniques that try to match words\nof queries with words of documents. The problem is that users want to retrieve on the basis of\nconceptual content, and individual words provide unreliable evidence about the conceptual topic or\nmeaning of a document. There are usually many ways to express a given concept, so the literal\nterms in a user’s query may not match those of a relevant document. In addition, most words ha",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Section 1 (Introduction) and the start of Section 2 (Deficiencies of current automatic indexing and retrieval methods). Because users search for conceptual content whereas literal words are unreliable indicators, Latent Semantic Indexing (LSI) treats observed term–document associations as noisy statistical data and applies SVD to construct a semantic space where closely associated terms and documents sit near one another, even when a term does not literally appear in a document. Section 2 identifies two core failure modes of literal term matching: synonymy (many ways to refer to the same concept, with two people choosing the same main keyword less than 20% of the time, hurting recall) and polysemy (words having multiple distinct meanings).",
        "state": "draft",
