      "content": "Deerwester\n- 2 -\n(homography). In different contexts or when used by different people the same term (e.g. \"chip\")\ntakes on varying referential signiﬁcance. Thus the use of a term in a search query does not\nnecessarily mean that a document containing or labeled by the same term is of interest. Polysemy is\none factor underlying poor \"precision\".\nThe failure of current automatic indexing to overcome these problems can be largely traced to\nthree factors. The ﬁrst factor is that the way index terms are identiﬁed is incomplete. The terms\nused to describe or index a document typically contain only a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continuation of Section 2 and opening of Section 3 (Rationale of the LSI method, Section 3.1 Illustration of retrieval problems). Polysemy (homography, such as \"chip\") degrades precision, and existing automatic indexing fails for three reasons: (1) incomplete index term identification (which thesauri or \"unlimited aliasing\" only partially address at the risk of ambiguity and scale limits), (2) lack of automatic polysemy disambiguation (controlled vocabularies are costly and Boolean intersections rely on users guessing exact limiting terms), and (3) treating word types as mutually independent, ignoring term co-occurrence redundancy. Section 3.1 introduces a fictional term-by-document matrix (Table 1) to illustrate these failures.",
