      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continuation of Section 3.2, contrasting LSI with prior factor-analytic and concept-space approaches and stating the first model selection criterion. Earlier factor-analytic retrieval experiments (e.g., Borko & Bernick, Ossorio) were limited by computational cost, low dimensionality or binary clustering reductions, tedious human similarity judgments, and one-mode representations that modeled only documents or only terms; Koll's joint concept space used only seven hand-chosen, non-orthogonal axes. The authors highlight four distinguishing features of their LSI approach—realistic dataset sizes (1,000–2,000 abstracts and 5,000–7,000 terms), ~100 dimensions, joint term-and-document representation, and direct unrotated retrieval—and state Criterion 1: adjustable representational richness (nk parameters in a k-dimensional model).",
