      "content": "Deerwester\n- 8 -\nparticular term, query, or document can be expressed by k factor values, or equivalently, by the\nlocation of a vector in the k -space deﬁned by the factors. The meaning representation is economical,\nin the sense that N original index terms have been replaced by the k <N best surrogates by which\nthey can be approximated. We make no attempt to interpret the underlying factors, nor to \"rotate\"\nthem to some meaningful orientation. Our aim is not to be able to describe the factors verbally but\nmerely to be able to represent terms, documents and queries in a way that escapes the unr",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continuation of Section 4.1 on dimensionality, query processing, and the Bellcore technical memoranda example. The k < N orthogonal factors are not rotated or verbally interpreted, and imperfect reconstruction of the raw term-by-document matrix is deliberate because discarding smaller singular values filters out noise while preserving reliable semantic associations; k is chosen operationally to maximize retrieval effectiveness rather than variance explained. Queries are represented as pseudo-documents (weighted sums of their term vectors) and ranked against documents by cosine similarity; a concrete example of 9 Bellcore technical memo titles (5 on human–computer interaction, c1–c5, and 4 on graph theory, m1–m4) is introduced.",
